.TH PCM_CONV 1 "2026" "fftrate 1.6.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pcm_conv \- convert WAVE files: change sample rate, bit depth, or channels
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pcm_conv
is a command-line WAVE file converter for audio production: change sample
rate, bit depth, or channel layout using high-quality FFT/DCT-based
resampling.
.P
Typical use: downsampling high-resolution DXD/DSF masters to a HiRes PCM
format suitable for playback on a given OS/DAC combination \(em e.g. 32-bit
integer 192kHz for Linux/ALSA, or 32-bit float 96kHz for macOS/CoreAudio.
.SH USAGE
.TP
Downsample a DXD/DSF-derived 32-bit/352.8kHz WAV to 32-bit/192kHz (Linux, FFT transform):
.B pcm_conv \-b 32 \-f 192000 \-T fft \-v master_352k8_32bit.wav out_192k_32bit_linux.wav
.TP
Downsample the same source to 32-bit float/96kHz (macOS):
.B pcm_conv \-b 32f \-f 96000 \-T fft \-v master_352k8_32bit.wav out_96k_32bitfloat_macos.wav
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pcm_conv
.RI [ input.wav ]\ [ output.wav ]
[options]
.SH EXAMPLE
Convert a 352800 Hz / 32-bit file down to 192000 Hz, using the FFT
transform, with verbose progress output:
.sp
.nf
$ pcm_conv \-f 192000 \-b 32 \-T fft \-v \e
    "Faust \- Funeral March Of A Marionette \- 32bit.wav" \e
    Faust_192kHz_32bit_downfft.wav

Input file : Faust \- Funeral March Of A Marionette \- 32bit.wav
 Samples rate    = 352800 Hz
 Channels        = 2
 Bits per sample = 32 (actual: 32, float)

Output file: Faust_192kHz_32bit_downfft.wav
 Samples rate    = 192000 Hz
 Channels        = 2
 Bits per sample = 32 (actual: 32, fixed)

Windows : "Vorbis" (37632 => 20480)
Delay   : 107 ms
Trans.  : "FFT"

[|||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||||]  99.0 %
Ok.

File duration  : 275.906667 sec
Processing time: 13.305000 sec ( 4.82% of real time)
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c \fInumber\fR
Output channels: 1=mono, 2=stereo, 3=2/1 surround, 4=2/2 quadro,
5=3/2 surround, 6=3/2+SW dolby.
.TP  
.B \-I \fIorder\fR  
Input channel order (comma-separated speaker list, e.g. FL,FR,FC,LFE,BL,BR,RL,RR).  
Use to match the channel order expected by the source's audio subsystem:  
.RS  
.IP \(bu 2  
OSS4: FL,FR,FC,LFE,BL,BR,RL,RR  
.IP \(bu 2  
ALSA: FL,FR,BL,BR,FC,LFE,RL,RR  
.IP \(bu 2  
MS/Windows: FL,FR,FC,LFE,BL,BR,RL,RR,BC,SL,SR,TC,TFL,TFC,TFR,TBL,TBC,TBR  
.RE  
.TP  
.B \-O \fIorder\fR  
Output channel order (same syntax/values as \-I), for matching the  
target playback subsystem's expected channel order.
.TP
.B \-b \fIbits\fR
Output bit depth: 8, 16 .. 32, or 32f/64f for floating point.
.TP
.B \-f \fIfrequency\fR
Output sample rate in Hz (tested from 6000 to 192000).
.TP
.B \-T \fItrans\fR
Transform: dct (default) or fft.
.TP
.B \-W \fIwin\fR
Window: vorbis (default), hanning, or rect.
.TP
.B \-v
Show verbose info while converting (like the example above).
.TP
.B \--help-all
Show all help information.
.TP
.B \--version
Show version information.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pcm_info (1),
.BR pcm_mse (1)
